WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.899
When Joaquin Phoenix won the Best Actor Oscar
unbelievably, his first for Joker at the 92nd

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.320
Academy Awards, it seemed a bit like he was
the only one who was surprised.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.320
The actor challenged himself physically, mentally,
and emotionally to bring us the best screen

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:15.880
performance of 2019.

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:21.460
Although Phoenix famously didn't look to any
previous screen interpretations of the Joker

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:26.080
including Heath Ledger's in crafting his performance,
he did share one aspect of the late Dark Knight

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.520
star's approach to the role.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:31.740
Phoenix found himself very isolated while
shooting the film, for a couple of reasons.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:35.870
For one, it was difficult for him to simply
turn off Arthur Fleck at the end of the day.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:36.660
"Man.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:45.080
You know what I'm talking about, it's so hard
to just try to be happy all the time."

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.300
In an interview with Metro, the actor said:

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.580
"At the end of the night there was never really
an end.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.800
It stayed with me every night after work for
another two or three hours.

00:00:52.801 --> 00:00:57.440
I never felt this moment of being done with
my work day [...] I didn't socialize at all.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.190
The person I interacted with was [director
Todd Phillips].

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:01.420
That became my world.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:02.480
It was nonstop."

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.690
Phoenix also revealed that as a by-product
of his own strict diet, his opportunities

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:10.470
to interact with the cast and crew were even
more limited than they might otherwise have

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:10.960
been.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:11.720
He said:

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.500
"I also didn't really have a life at that
time because I couldn't go out to eat."

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:20.160
Fortunately, the role was simply so stimulating
for Phoenix that he actually found these conditions

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:21.040
to be agreeable.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:21.600
He said:

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:23.440
"I want to say it was super hard.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:27.510
But it was so inspiring, that as much energy
I put into it, it gave it back.

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:30.720
I would go home after work and we would just
talk about the next scene.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.260
I was just super excited.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:36.300
I would show up at set two hours early to
just sit in the trailer and talk about it."

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.440
Phoenix famously lost over 50 pounds for the
role of Arthur Fleck.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:45.580
In a wide-ranging talk with USA Today about
Joker, he revealed that he subsisted largely

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:49.580
on a diet of steamed green beans, apples,
and lettuce during the shoot.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:50.760
This was nothing new.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.000
He had lost a similar amount of weight for
his role in Paul Thomas Anderson's acclaimed

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.640
2012 drama The Master.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:00.060
Just because he had done it before, though,
doesn't mean that he was in any way keen to

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.060
repeat the experience.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:05.860
Phoenix, who disclosed that the same nutritionist
tasked with whittling him down for The Master

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.300
returned to torment him further for Joker,
said:

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.720
"In all honesty, I didn't want to do it again.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:14.900
It's a horrible, brutal diet, but you get
all the vitamins and minerals, so you're like

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:15.400
safe.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:16.520
It's grotesque."

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.420
Phoenix has been open about the fact that
the dramatic weight loss had a profound effect

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:24.519
on his mental state, and that he was able
to use the psychological difficulty it presented

00:02:24.519 --> 00:02:26.019
to inform his acting in Joker.

00:02:26.019 --> 00:02:30.209
If he had been able to have it his way, though,
Arthur wouldn't have been the rail-thin, emaciated

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:31.709
figure we see in the film.

00:02:31.709 --> 00:02:35.530
In an interview a month before the film's
release, the actor told the New York Times:

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.000
"I [thought] he should be kind of heavy.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.659
Todd was like, 'I think you should do the
real thin person.'"

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:44.120
His assessment of the experience didn't soften
over the months after Joker hit theaters;

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:48.099
he told the Times that sticking to the diet
was, quote, "a horrible way to live."

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:53.269
Finding his version of the Joker's famous
cackle was a particularly daunting task for

00:02:53.269 --> 00:02:56.900
Phoenix, as the film posited a unique take
on that particular trait.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.939
Arthur's laughter is pathological, a disorder
which causes him to burst into violent, uncontrollable

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:04.440
laughter at the most inappropriate of times.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:09.480
“haha”

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:16.579
Part of Phoenix's method for perfecting his
version of the Joker's laugh was to study

00:03:16.579 --> 00:03:20.500
videos of real-life mental patients afflicted
with this disorder.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:24.819
For a time, the actor was reluctant to employ
the laugh, as he simply wasn't confident that

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:25.520
he'd nailed it.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:26.020
He explained:

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.819
"I think Joker is a part of [Arthur] that's
trying to emerge, and I think that was a really

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:31.500
interesting way of looking at this laugh.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.920
But honestly, I didn't think that I could
do it.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:37.700
I would practice alone, and then asked Todd
to come over to audition my laugh, because

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.940
I felt like I had to do it on the spot and
in front of somebody else.

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:42.280
It took me a long time."

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.900
Phoenix has even said that, to a large extent,
he's still not sure if he got it exactly right

00:03:46.909 --> 00:03:48.640
except in a few instances.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.469
Speaking with ETOnline, he said:

00:03:50.469 --> 00:03:55.200
"I thought at some point that it would become
easier, but I don't think it did.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.000
I think it became more difficult, actually."

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:01.440
The actor went on to explain that while it
worked for some scenes, others were, quote,

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:02.080
"a struggle," saying:

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.859
“Sometimes one take would work and another
wouldn't.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:08.720
I think it was something that was alive, in
a way."

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:13.480
Perhaps the biggest challenge for Phoenix
in preparing to play Arthur Fleck was also,

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:17.120
in large part, what drew him to the role:
an extreme sense of uncertainty.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:21.540
In the USA Today interview, the actor explained
that the character has an innate sense of

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:26.520
slipperiness to his identity, a resolute unwillingness
to allow himself to be pinned down as any

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:27.480
one archetype.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:32.190
This quality exhibited itself not only on
the pages of the screenplay, but on set, as

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:35.460
Arthur's characterization morphed and evolved
throughout Joker's shoot.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:36.240
Phoenix said:

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:40.440
"When we were prepping for it, I felt very
frustrated because I couldn't lock on anything

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.440
that felt like a foundation for the character.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:45.360
And at some point, I realized that was the
f--ing point.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:46.360
He was unstable."

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:55.540
"Gary, you were the only one who was ever
nice to me."

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:59.940
Eventually, though, Phoenix along with Phillips
and the rest of Joker's cast and crew found

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:02.440
that this grey area was exactly where they
needed to be.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:02.940
He said:

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:04.520
"It's shaky ground as an actor.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:08.840
I enjoy not knowing precisely what a character
may do, but you want to have like a couple

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.360
moments that you feel solid about.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.020
And that never really happened.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:14.290
We just became very comfortable with not knowing."

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:18.471
It was a singularly unique approach which
led to a singularly unique performance, one

00:05:18.471 --> 00:05:22.590
which finally netted Phoenix that golden statue
which he probably should have been awarded

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:23.340
years ago.

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:25.400
In his acceptance speech, he told his peers:

00:05:25.409 --> 00:05:28.139
"We share the same love that's the love of
film.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:31.199
And this form of expression has given me the
most extraordinary life.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:32.860
I don't know where I'd be without it."

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:37.160
It's only fitting that this is so, because
Phoenix has given us nothing but extraordinary

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:40.340
performances and with Joker, an all-time great
one.

